From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 04:52:53 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] kosmickrisp: restart the render encoder for layered draws

A Metal render command encoder that has already run one draw drops most of
the primitives of a later draw that writes a different
render_target_array_index. The layer the first draw wrote stays correct and
the primitives are not misrouted, they simply never land: the newly selected
layer keeps whatever the attachment held when the pass began.

The loss is deterministic and large. A layered pass drawing 1048576 points
per layer into a 1024x1024x2 R32G32B32A32_SFLOAT attachment loses the first
908672 points of the second draw; triangles lose 933472 of 1048576 covered
texels, and three- and four-layer passes lose the same amount on every layer
after the first. This is what fails
dEQP-VK.api.copy_and_blit.core.use_after_copy.*.transfer_dst_optimal.1024x1024x2*_color_att_flag_img2img:
the copy itself is correct, and it is the layered pass that samples the copy
result afterwards that loses its second layer.

None of the documented requirements for layered rendering are unmet:
inputPrimitiveTopology is set from the pipeline's topology class,
renderTargetArrayLength is the attachment layer count, and the generated MSL
declares and writes [[render_target_array_index]]. Neither an encoder-scope
nor a queue-scope barrier between the draws helps, and neither does dropping
the single-view setVertexAmplificationCount call or clearing
allowGPUOptimizedContents. Only ending the encoder and starting a new one
recovers the primitives.

So restart the render encoder before every Metal draw that is not the first
in the current encoder whenever the pass is layered and the stage that feeds
the rasterizer writes the layer: the emulated geometry shader's raster draw,
else the tessellation evaluation shader, else the vertex shader. The decision
is made per Metal draw rather than per draw call, because each entry of a
multi-draw is a Metal draw of its own. The restart runs through the existing
render-pass flush path, which forces the attachment load, so nothing is lost;
the cost is one attachment load and store per such draw, and a pass that is not
layered or never writes the layer is untouched.

A restart has to preserve everything the open encoder was carrying. Vertex
amplification did not survive it: vkCmdClearAttachments turns amplification
off for its meta draw, which routes its own layers, and cs_start_render
re-derived the mapping from the view mask, so a restart while the meta draw
was being flushed handed it the pass's multiview mapping instead. In a
multiview pass that put the clear on the wrong layers, which is what
dEQP-VK.multiview.dynamic_rendering.clear_attachments.no_queries.5_10_5_10
caught: view mask 10's clear landed on layers 0 and 2. Record the
amplification mapping in the graphics state and let every encoder start
replay it, so a mid-pass restart keeps the mapping the pass, or the clear,
installed.

The workaround is deliberately not gated on a draw size. Splitting the same
1048576 points into four draws of 262144 still loses 653984 of them, so there
is no primitive count below which a later draw is safe; a threshold of 262144
would have left most of the failure in place. A CLEAR load op on the same pass
does render correctly, but that rests on a single configuration and three
earlier narrowings of this failure were each refuted by the next measurement,
so it is recorded in the workaround documentation rather than used as a gate.

kosmickrisp_layered_draw_sequence reproduces the primitive loss without the
workaround, kosmickrisp_layered_draw_multi_draw and
kosmickrisp_layered_draw_geometry reproduce the same 908672-point loss through
a two-entry multi-draw and through a layer a geometry shader writes, and
kosmickrisp_multiview_clear_attachments reproduces the misrouted multiview
clear without the recorded amplification mapping.
